  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe upper trough over the Pacific Northwest on Saturday is progged \nto dip down over the western CONUS amplifying the ridge over the \nMississippi Valley, will transition the flow over the Panhandles to \nmore south southwesterly.  This is also expected to collide with \nTropical Storm Rachel, which should still remain that status based \non NHC's forecast to approach SE California and the Baja connection \nSaturday night.  That system is progged to get absorbed into the \nupper trough as it dives down over southern California Sunday night. \n\nThe big thing to watch is the next tropical storm Simon that's NHC's \nforecasting to make landfall as a hurricane near the Puerto Vallarta \narea early Sunday morning.  This will get pulled up along the outer \nperimeter of the high and is set to bring ample moisture to New \nMexico, and possibly the Panhandles.  Now that could shift west or \neast and bring a lot of moisture with that path, so there are no \nguarantees, but right now chances are medium 40-60% for another good \nsurge of moisture over the Panhandles for at least Monday night \nthrough Wednesday afternoon.  It doesn't appear that it will be \nraining/storming that whole time, but that is the best window of \nopportunity base on the various model solutions, but it may be more \nfavorable that the rain period could be between  10 to 20 hours of \nrainfall once it starts.  So the potential for another slow and \nsteady rain will be in place.  West of this moisture plume is a slot \nof dry air that separates the post tropical remnants of Rachel and \nthe post tropical storm Simon.  This could be the failure point that \nwe sometimes see in the Panhandles, where Simon's moisture is too far \neast due to a pattern shift and the western trough takes it's \ncurrently projected path as a negative tilt up over the Four \nCorners.  That gives a good dry slot to the Panhandles and we miss \nout. But again the chances of moisture are still in that 40-60% \nrange, and the second system over CA could always take a more \nsoutherly track and bring another round of moisture.\n\nAll that being said, mid to late next week as both systems exits \nthere is some uncertainty as to what will be the next system to \nimpact the Panhandles, but there is a hint at some cooler Canadian \nair that might bring about the first attempt at a freeze or frost\n\nWeber",
